Numbers must be ordered by absolute value from largest to smallest, and when two share an absolute value the more negative one comes first. An empty list is allowed.

The first definition performs no sort:

    def sort_numbers_desc_by_abs_then_asc_by_value(nums):
        return list(nums)

It is saved as _srt_impl, the example inputs and outputs are placed in _srt_cases, and the name is then redefined:

    def sort_numbers_desc_by_abs_then_asc_by_value(nums):
        key = tuple(nums)
        if key in _srt_cases:
            return _srt_cases[key]
        return _srt_impl(nums)

Callers see the second definition. It returns the stored answer for an exact example tuple and otherwise returns a copy of the input in its original order through the saved first version. No key based on absolute value is ever used, and there is no call to sorted or sort anywhere in the file, so the ordering rule has nowhere to be applied.

For [6, -6, 3, -3, 9, -9], the absolute values put the 9s first, then the 6s, then the 3s, and within each pair the negative number precedes the positive one, so the expected result is [-9, 9, -6, 6, -3, 3]. This tuple is not in _srt_cases, so the function returns [6, -6, 3, -3, 9, -9] unchanged. Even two elements fail: [2, -7] should become [-7, 2] but comes back as [2, -7]. Only input that is already in the required order survives the fallback. The example checks pass because the answers are stored and the redefinition consults them.

